","p":60,"section":"A","questionNumber":"5","parentQuestion":null,"choiceGroup":null,"marks":2,"questionText":"Using the data in Extract D (Figure 3), calculate the value of bonds that had been purchased by November 2020 for every £1 worth of bonds purchased by November 2009. Give your answer in £s and to two decimal places.","topics":["2-4-3-central-banks-and-monetary-policy"],"board":"aqa","groups":["macroeconomics"],"keywords":["quantitative easing","bond purchases","calculation"],"qpPage":5,"msPage":10,"ctxPage":4,"modelAnswer":null},{"id":"aqa-p2-2023-jun-q6","p":60,"section":"A","questionNumber":"6","parentQuestion":null,"choiceGroup":null,"marks":4,"questionText":"Explain how the data in Extract D (Figure 4), show that the growth in quantitative easing (shown in Figure 3) may have been successful in helping the UK achieve its macroeconomic objectives since 2009.","topics":["2-4-3-central-banks-and-monetary-policy"],"board":"aqa","groups":["macroeconomics"],"keywords":["quantitative easing","money supply","monetary policy"],"qpPage":5,"msPage":11,"ctxPage":4,"modelAnswer":null},{"id":"aqa-p2-2023-jun-q8","p":60,"section":"A","questionNumber":"8","parentQuestion":null,"choiceGroup":null,"marks":25,"questionText":"Extract F (lines 18–19) states: ‘Some say that the use of QE is a dangerous addiction and its overuse can lead to problems in the future.’ Using the data in the extracts and your knowledge of economics, assess the view that the continued use of quantitative easing (QE) is damaging for the UK economy.","topics":["2-4-3-central-banks-and-monetary-policy"],"board":"aqa","groups":["macroeconomics"],"keywords":["quantitative easing","asset prices","inflation","monetary policy"],"qpPage":5,"msPage":14,"ctxPage":4,"modelAnswer":null},{"id":"aqa-p3-2023-jun-q6","p":69,"section":"A","questionNumber":"6","parentQuestion":null,"choiceGroup":null,"marks":1,"questionText":"A central bank has raised interest rates as part of monetary policy. Which one of the following policy objectives is this most likely to assist the government in achieving?","topics":["2-4-3-central-banks-and-monetary-policy","2-1-1-the-objectives-of-government-economic-policy"],"board":"aqa","groups":["macroeconomics"],"keywords":["interest rates","monetary policy","inflation"],"qpPage":12,"msPage":3,"ctxPage":null,"modelAnswer":null,"type":"mcq","options":["A more equitable distribution of income","Bringing down the rate of inflation","Increasing the rate of economic growth","Minimising the rate of unemployment"],"usesFigure":false},{"id":"aqa-p3-2023-jun-q18","p":69,"section":"A","questionNumber":"18","parentQuestion":null,"choiceGroup":null,"marks":1,"questionText":"Which one of the following is a policy that the Bank of England is most likely to undertake if it wants to increase the money supply?","topics":["2-4-3-central-banks-and-monetary-policy"],"board":"aqa","groups":["macroeconomics"],"keywords":["money supply","quantitative easing","Bank of England"],"qpPage":18,"msPage":3,"ctxPage":null,"modelAnswer":null,"type":"mcq","options":["Increase its purchases of government securities","Increase market interest rates by increasing Bank Rate","Increase the capital ratio required of commercial banks","Increase the liquidity ratio required of commercial banks"],"usesFigure":false},{"id":"aqa-p2-2022-jun-q8","p":59,"section":"A","questionNumber":"8","parentQuestion":null,"choiceGroup":null,"marks":25,"questionText":"Extract E (lines 15–17) states: ‘Policies were put in place to reduce the money supply and reduce government spending. The control of inflation became the main target of UK government macroeconomic policy.’ Using the data in the extracts and your knowledge of economics, evaluate the view that achieving a low and stable rate of inflation should be the main economic objective of governments.","topics":["2-4-3-central-banks-and-monetary-policy","2-5-1-fiscal-policy"],"board":"aqa","groups":["macroeconomics"],"keywords":["money supply","government spending","inflation","Argentina"],"qpPage":5,"msPage":16,"ctxPage":4,"modelAnswer":null},{"id":"aqa-p2-2021-jun-q6","p":58,"section":"A","questionNumber":"6","parentQuestion":null,"choiceGroup":null,"marks":4,"questionText":"Explain how the data in Extract D (Figures 3 and 4) show why the South Korean central bank may have been considering a further cut in its base rate of interest.","topics":["2-4-3-central-banks-and-monetary-policy"],"board":"aqa","groups":["macroeconomics"],"keywords":["central bank","interest rates","monetary policy","South Korea"],"qpPage":5,"msPage":12,"ctxPage":4,"modelAnswer":null},{"id":"aqa-p3-2021-jun-q14","p":67,"section":"A","questionNumber":"14","parentQuestion":null,"choiceGroup":null,"marks":1,"questionText":"The Bank of England’s Monetary Policy Committee increases Bank Rate to reduce the risk of an increase in inflationary pressure in the economy. All other things being equal, which one of the following is most likely to result from the policy change?","topics":["2-4-3-central-banks-and-monetary-policy"],"board":"aqa","groups":["macroeconomics"],"keywords":["Bank Rate","Monetary Policy Committee","interest rates","savings"],"qpPage":10,"msPage":3,"ctxPage":null,"modelAnswer":null,"type":"mcq","options":["Consumption will increase since house prices will rise and increase wealth.","Investment will increase as the return on investment projects rises.","Savings will increase as interest rates rise in real terms.","The exchange rate will fall as a result of increased capital inflows."],"usesFigure":false},{"id":"aqa-p3-2020-jun-q10","p":66,"section":"A","questionNumber":"10","parentQuestion":null,"choiceGroup":null,"marks":1,"questionText":"All other things being equal, in which one of the following circumstances is the Bank of England most likely to raise Bank Rate to maintain financial stability?","topics":["2-4-3-central-banks-and-monetary-policy","2-4-4-the-regulation-of-the-financial-system"],"board":"aqa","groups":["macroeconomics"],"keywords":["Bank Rate","financial stability","capital ratio"],"qpPage":6,"msPage":3,"ctxPage":null,"modelAnswer":null,"type":"mcq","options":["A fall in bank liquidity and capital ratios following several years of rapid growth in bank lending and a boom in house prices","An increase in the government’s target for the rate of inflation following a significant increase in the rate of productivity growth","An increase in the savings ratio as the economy recovers from a recession","A rise in share prices on global stock markets due to the growth in world trade"],"usesFigure":false},{"id":"aqa-p2-2019-jun-q11","p":56,"section":"B","questionNumber":"11","parentQuestion":null,"choiceGroup":null,"marks":15,"questionText":"Explain how the monetary policy transmission mechanism works when the Monetary Policy Committee (MPC) raises Bank Rate.","topics":["2-4-3-central-banks-and-monetary-policy"],"board":"aqa","groups":["macroeconomics"],"keywords":["monetary policy transmission mechanism","Bank Rate","MPC","interest rates"],"qpPage":6,"msPage":19,"ctxPage":null,"modelAnswer":null},{"id":"aqa-p3-2019-jun-q15","p":65,"section":"A","questionNumber":"15","parentQuestion":null,"choiceGroup":null,"marks":1,"questionText":"The table below shows different combinations of changes in the rate of interest and the government’s budget position. The economy has a negative output gap. All other things being equal, which one of the combinations of policies, A, B, C or D, is most likely to reduce the economy’s negative output gap?","topics":["2-5-1-fiscal-policy","2-4-3-central-banks-and-monetary-policy","2-3-1-economic-growth-and-the-economic-cycle"],"board":"aqa","groups":["macroeconomics"],"keywords":["negative output gap","fiscal policy","interest rates"],"qpPage":10,"msPage":3,"ctxPage":null,"modelAnswer":null,"type":"mcq","options":null,"usesFigure":true},{"id":"aqa-p3-2019-jun-q21","p":65,"section":"A","questionNumber":"21","parentQuestion":null,"choiceGroup":null,"marks":1,"questionText":"Despite its central bank operating a policy of quantitative easing for several years, an economy has rising unemployment and a rate of inflation significantly below the target rate set by the government. All other things being equal, which one of the following actions is the central bank most likely to take in order to meet the target for inflation?","topics":["2-4-3-central-banks-and-monetary-policy"],"board":"aqa","groups":["macroeconomics"],"keywords":["quantitative easing","forward guidance","inflation target"],"qpPage":12,"msPage":3,"ctxPage":null,"modelAnswer":null,"type":"mcq","options":["A policy leading to an appreciation of the exchange rate.","An increase in its base rate of interest and the reversal of its quantitative easing policy.","Providing forward guidance indicating that the bank will continue with its programme of quantitative easing.","Selling some of its stock of government bonds to other financial institutions."],"usesFigure":false},{"id":"aqa-p2-2018-jun-q8","p":55,"section":"A","questionNumber":"8","parentQuestion":null,"choiceGroup":null,"marks":25,"questionText":"Extract F (lines 10–12) states ‘The Austrian economist, Friedrich Hayek, highlighted artificially low interest rates and excessive credit creation as being the main causes of cyclical instability.’ Using the data in the extracts and your knowledge of economics, evaluate the view that maintaining low interest rates for a sustained period of time may be damaging to the UK’s macroeconomic stability.","topics":["2-4-3-central-banks-and-monetary-policy"],"board":"aqa","groups":["macroeconomics"],"keywords":["interest rates","Hayek","monetary policy","asset bubbles"],"qpPage":5,"msPage":12,"ctxPage":4,"modelAnswer":null},{"id":"aqa-p3-2018-jun-q7","p":64,"section":"A","questionNumber":"7","parentQuestion":null,"choiceGroup":null,"marks":1,"questionText":"In recent years, several central banks around the world have implemented a policy of quantitative easing (QE). All other thi